{
  "manufacturer": "Rotork",
  "manufacturer_slug": "rotork",
  "model": "Modbus RTU Mk3 option card (IQ3, SI3/SI4, CK, CVA, CMA, K-range)",
  "model_slug": "modbus-rtu-mk3",
  "short_model": "Rotork Modbus Mk3",
  "aliases": [
    "IQ3 Modbus",
    "Rotork MFU Mk3",
    "Modbus Mk3 module",
    "SI3 Modbus",
    "SI4 Modbus",
    "CK Modbus",
    "CVA Modbus",
    "CMA Modbus",
    "K-range Modbus"
  ],
  "equipment_type": "Actuator / valve",
  "applies_to": "Rotork Modbus RTU Mk3 option module (MFU) in IQ3, SI3, SI4, CK, CVA (firmware CVA1.14 or later), CMA and K-range actuators, using the default Rotork database (register 80 = 0), per PUB091-004-00 issue 08/25.",
  "summary": "The Mk3 Modbus module puts a Rotork electric valve actuator on an RS-485 Modbus RTU highway (single, dual or repeater versions). Open, close, stop, ESD and partial stroke can be sent as coils or as one register value, and position control uses a 0-100.0 % set-point register. Status bits, torque and valve position can be read as discrete inputs, input registers or holding registers, and holding registers 7-31 hold the positioner, comms-loss and network settings.",
  "transport": [
    "RTU"
  ],
  "defaults": {
    "unit_id": 247,
    "baud": 9600,
    "parity": "None",
    "data_bits": 8,
    "stop_bits": 1,
    "tcp_port": null
  },
  "addressing": {
    "convention": "0-based",
    "note": "The manual says every coil, discrete and register location is numbered from 0 with no offset, exactly as sent on the wire. Hosts that use 1/10001/30001/40001 numbering must add those offsets themselves."
  },
  "default_word_order": "CDAB",
  "firmware": "Modbus module firmware V104 or later",
  "source": {
    "title": "Modbus RTU Mk3 Option Technical Manual",
    "doc_number": "PUB091-004-00 Issue 08/25",
    "revision_date": "2025-08",
    "url": "https://media.rotork.com/api/public/content/pub091-004-000414-pdf-rtkimportasseteb0d51.pdf",
    "pages": "pp. 35-36 (comms spec), 37-49 (database, function codes, offsets, data locations), 50-51 (summary), 52-58 (configuration registers)"
  },
  "notes": [
    "Only two 32-bit values exist (motor start counts); the manual labels the low order word at the lower register and the high order word at the next one, so they are CDAB.",
    "Holding registers 0-4 and input registers 0-4 carry the same data; registers 0 and 1 pack the same bits as discrete inputs 0-29 (bit 0/15 excepted, see below).",
    "Ranges are printed in hex: torque 0-0x78 (0-120 %), position 0-0x3E8 (0.0-100.0 %).",
    "Data logger registers 32-59 update a short time after the actuator stops in Remote and can lag by up to two minutes. On ELB units registers 32-49 hold pressure and alarm data instead; the ELB-only tables are not included here.",
    "The comms fault timer (register 21) is given as 0-255 s in the table but 0-65 s in the text, and the Comms Lost Position text refers to 'Fault Mode (Register 1)' where the table uses register 7. Check on site.",
    "Not every actuator range supports every location (e.g. DO-5 to DO-8 are IQ3 only); see the manual's database summary table.",
    "Also supported: function 07 (exception status byte), 08 (loopback, sub-codes 00 and 02) and 17 (report slave ID: name, software version and tag).",
    "Status bit 0/15 (discrete input 15) is left out: the data tables call it EEPROM checksum failure, but the database summary and the signal descriptions call it Watchdog Recovery.",
    "Register 80 is marked read-only in the data logger table but writable (06) in the configuration register table; it is kept as RW."
  ],
